{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-\u00e6on/112616-freeform-psi-in-upcoming-books","id":112616,"title":"Freeform Psi in upcoming books?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","The Trinity Continuum","Trinity Continuum: \u00c6on"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-04-14T07:19:10-07:00","id":112616,"title":"Freeform Psi in upcoming books?","content":"I've just finished running my first couple Trinity games (a one-shot scouting party from a French rayonnaire village, a two-shot dungeon-esque crawl through Karoo during the first Chromatic attack, and a three-shot Nor\u00e7a vs Orgotek espionage game on Mujukuu) I've been using the Freeform Psi system front the Trinity Players Guide. My players love it. Some of them are old Mage fans and love the creativity gained through structure and limits. Some are newish to the d10 systems but love it for enhancing their own story in flexible ways. So is there a decent chance the freeform powers system is going to make an appearance in the new Continuum material? I had a great time puzzling out how to make it work, but some of the original stuff would be pretty confusing for a storyteller to pick up cold.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2732-darprime","name":"DarPrime","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-14T11:35:23-07:00","id":112808,"title":"","content":"The new books are using a different mechanics system altogether.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><span style=\"color:#800000\">Ian A. A. Watson</span></b><span style=\"color:#bdc3c7\">(he/she)</span><br /> <b>Onyx Path Community Manager<br /> Trinity Continuum Content Lead</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4-ianwatson","name":"IanWatson","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4_2.png","title":"Community Manager","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1512"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-15T03:32:38-07:00","id":113225,"title":"","content":"So, will it offend the NDA gods if I ask is it closer to the freeform system? I really enjoyed that system as well, although for games where psi was not expected to be a central part of thigns I generally went with the core book system.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1458-newk","name":"NewK","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"360"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-15T10:53:15-07:00","id":113425,"title":"","content":"They're still working on the rules so its probably a bit early for details. <br /> <br /> In a thread over on RPG.net there was some suggestion that there was a desire for more flexibility than the core book original rules provided, but not necessarily along the lines of the freeform rules - which had a bunch of issues. Seems (at least according to the interwebs) the freeform rules were not overly popular (I count myself in the group not being a fan of them - too messy).<br /> <br /> Somewhere in the middle then, perhaps?<br /> <br /> Google forums.rpg.net Aeon Continuum and there's a handful of threads if you want to track them down <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#ff0000\"><b>Ad Astra</b></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/232-chill","name":"CHILL","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar232_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"248"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-16T00:25:59-07:00","id":113894,"title":"","content":"If it's convenient that'd be nice. My big problem with the freeform rules and this comes as a fan of them, is that they required investment and rules knowledge by both GM and player. Not every player likes that (and that's acceptable of course, as playing styles vary). Unfortunately if you have a minxed group-- someone really likes the free form rules, letting them play with them creates something of a rules related power boost cmopared to someone who likes the simplicity of the base system.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1458-newk","name":"NewK","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"360"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-17T12:05:46-07:00","id":114861,"title":"","content":"Oh yeah, I hear you. <br /> <br /> I haven't looked at the in many years, but that feels familiar and is probably why I gave up on them after a few tests.<br /> <br /> I didn't have too much of an issue with the relatively low power levels in Aeon/Trinity as written, as it made for less reliance on 'mad powerz' and a more interesting game. But, yeah they definitely need to scale better and moderately more flexibility shouldn't hurt.<br /> <br /> At the risk of getting a bit tangential here, as a GM I prefer streamlined rules that keep things moving - the less discussion about how something works / checking in the rulebooks the better.<br /> <br /> My one (mild) gripe about Trinity 1st Ed was having to remember the various costs of each power whenever a player without a book asked about them. Thankfully, my players usually each took the time to remember the rules for a particular Mode they tended to prefer to use, so I had a bunch of insta-recall assistants on tap for the most part <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Those Modes had little internal consistency and I never had the time to memorize every little detail. I usually winged it with NPC's as I went, which worked well.<br /> <br /> Also, actually looking through the rules for powers was a bit of a chore in game in terms of finding information - having to separate fluff from crunch to get to the bits needed in the middle of a scene was.....irritating (yay for my insta-recall players!). <br /> <br /> That's 90's game design for you though. Thankfully we've moved on since then, as has OPP, if their more recent nWOD publications are any indication <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#ff0000\"><b>Ad Astra</b></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/232-chill","name":"CHILL","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar232_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"248"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-09T12:13:38-07:00","id":163877,"title":"","content":"Frankly I'd hope for something kinda like a small &quot;skill&quot; tree with each dot in the mode offering up 2 branches. You then run the middle path of the design philosophies imho.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Man's unfailing capacity to believe what he prefers to be true rather than what the evidence shows to be likely and possible has always astounded me. We long for a caring Universe which will save us from our childish mistakes, and in the face of mountains of evidence to the contrary we will pin all our hopes on the slimmest of doubts. God has not been proven not to exist, therefore he must exist.</i> <b>--Academician Prokhor Zakharov</b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3246-galaxy-master-zhad","name":"Galaxy Master Zhad","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3246_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"37"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-25T22:53:41-07:00","id":180459,"title":"","content":"The core rules are still under development, so all statements about the psi rules are purely speculative, but like Ian, I'm not remotely a fan of the freeform psi rules from the PG - I consider them to be too complex to ever consider using. The goal for the new rules is for them be both flexible and simple.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/96-john-snead","name":"John Snead","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar96_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"34"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}